  "tour": {
    "title": "From Death to Resurrection: What Happens to the Body?",
    "intro": "This tour follows the New Testament’s sequence rather than collapsing everything into “going to heaven”: first, what resurrection means; then the believer’s presence with Christ after death; then the promised transformation of the body; and finally the Bible’s picture of God’s renewed creation. The teachers sometimes use different emphases, so listen for the distinction between the intermediate state and the final hope.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:6a253c6-com-651c11dc-e7c9-3e9c-8d28-dfdec7d811d5",
        "startSec": 694,
        "endSec": 867,
        "why": "Begin here to clear up the central vocabulary problem. N.T. Wright says plainly, “Resurrection is not about somebody going into a glorious angelic state” and that the biblical words “simply aren't about going to heaven”; he then connects Jesus’ recognizable, wounded body with God giving each person a new body. This gives the tour its basic category: resurrection is bodily renewal, not merely survival after death.",
        "episodeTitle": "#164 Easter questions on the resurrection of Jesus",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR6878813537.mp3?updated=1749655811",
        "durationSec": 2012,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:heaven-earth-part-3",
        "startSec": 2040,
        "endSec": 2134,
        "why": "This short clip answers the question about the interval between death and resurrection. The speakers gather Paul’s language—“if I'm away from my body, I'm at home with the Lord”—and conclude, “What happens after you die? You're with Jesus,” while stressing that Paul does not view this as the permanent destination because “the whole point is new creation.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Jesus, The Ultimate Heaven & Earth Meeting Place",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/9327a394-42a0-4eaf-a203-09e63e82aacd/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=9327a394-42a0-4eaf-a203-09e63e82aacd",
        "durationSec": 2514,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:7dbd0c70-com-ad14c96d-7e81-3bdd-a910-a23bb2f5b5f9",
        "startSec": 603,
        "endSec": 899,
        "why": "Now listen to an exposition of 2 Corinthians 5, where the speaker distinguishes the present “earthy tent” from the future body. The key clarification is that Christians do not ultimately want “the soul to be freed from the body”; Paul’s hope is to be “clothed with a glorious, heavenly, immortal body.” This holds together the real comfort of being with Christ and the stronger hope of bodily resurrection.",
        "episodeTitle": "2 Corinthians 5:1-10",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/jckmzx/2_corinthians_51-10816em.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1584,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:8380ffa3-com-b72975ac-389f-3ffa-84fb-478fb64f2873",
        "startSec": 1191,
        "endSec": 1480,
        "why": "This is the tour’s most direct treatment of what the resurrection body is like. The speaker explains that “spiritual” in 1 Corinthians 15 “doesn't mean immaterial,” but means a body empowered and fitted for life in God’s coming world. He then identifies Jesus’ resurrection body as the pattern: “Our resurrection body will be like Jesus' resurrection body.”",
        "episodeTitle": "1 Corinthians 15:29-58",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/d8ngn3/1_corinthians_1529-589yhwk.mp3",
        "durationSec": 2010,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:b65bd89d-com-e0129c8f-9f93-392f-bcba-0def335d7c00",
        "startSec": 300,
        "endSec": 556,
        "why": "End with the New Testament’s final horizon in Revelation 21. Rather than picturing God’s people permanently escaping upward, the speaker emphasizes that “it's not God's people going up into heaven, but the new Jerusalem coming down out of heaven,” with God dwelling among them and death, mourning, crying, and pain removed. This gives the bodily resurrection its setting: renewed creation and restored communion with God.",
        "episodeTitle": "Revelation 21:1-22:5",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/bwuev4wq3hdtt93e/revelation_211-2259g6v5.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1707,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips describe a two-stage hope: after death, believers are with Christ; at the resurrection, God transforms and raises the body; and the final biblical picture is not disembodied existence in the clouds but God dwelling with his people in the renewed creation. Jesus’ empty tomb and recognizable yet glorified body are the pattern and promise behind that hope.",
    "totalSeconds": 1108
  },
